Add the settings module of the KDecoration3 window decoration

The KDecoration3 decoration of the previous patch reads the kwinqtcurverc file
of the KDE4 decoration. Its plugin metadata names no configuration module, so
the pencil button of System Settings > Window Decorations opens nothing.
qt6/kwinconfig builds the standalone module for a Qt4 build alone, but
qt6/config compiles the same page into kstyle_qtcurve6_config.so. The settings
dialogue of the QtCurve style therefore writes the file today. That page sits
behind the style, and not behind the window frame. The pencil button of the
Window Decorations page still needs a target on the KWin side.

The patch adds qt6/kdecoration/config, a KCMUtils plugin with the name
kcm_qtcurvedecoration. kcoreaddons_add_plugin() installs the plugin in the
org.kde.kdecoration3.kcm directory of the Qt plugin path, beside
kcm_breezedecoration.so. kcmutils_generate_desktop_file() installs
kcm_qtcurvedecoration.desktop, so the search of System Settings finds the page.
qt6/kdecoration/qtcurve.json gets the X-KDE-ConfigModule key that breeze.json
holds, and KWin opens the module from the pencil button.

Two pages write the file, and the design of this patch cannot drop either one.
The page inside kstyle_qtcurve6_config.so writes a shadow key while the custom
shadows box is on alone. It writes BorderSize, BorderlessMax, Grouping and the
three opacity keys as well. The new module writes every shadow key on every
save, except the ShadowType key and the Color key. It writes the Color key for
the CT_CUSTOM colour type alone, and it removes that key for every other type.

The new module writes no ShadowType key. QtCurve::ShadowConfig reads the key,
and the KWin page of kstyle_qtcurve6_config reads the value back into its own
box. A user can hold both pages open, and the last save wins. load() reads the
file again before it fills the widgets, so a save from the other page reaches an
open module. The doc of kwinConfigFile, the class doc of DecorationKCM and the
class doc of DecorationSettings carry the warning.

QtCurve::DecorationKCM is the KCModule. QtCurve::DecorationSettings and
QtCurve::ShadowSettings read and write the file. The module edits the keys of
the file that change the decoration:

* The General group holds OuterBorder, InnerBorder, RoundBottom, CustomShadows,
  TitleBarPad and EdgePad.
* The ActiveShadows group and the InactiveShadows group hold Size, HOffset,
  VOffset, ColorType and Color.

The module writes the Color key for the CT_CUSTOM colour type alone, because the
decoration reads the key for that type alone. It removes that key for every
other type. The module keeps every other key
of the file, so the keys of the KDE4 decoration stay: BorderSize, BorderlessMax,
Grouping, ActiveOpacity, InactiveOpacity, OpaqueBorder and ShadowType. The
module edits no key of the configuration file of the QtCurve style. The settings
dialogue of the style still holds the title bar gradient, the button layout and
the caption alignment.

One source of truth for the keys. qt6/kdecoration/qtcurvedecorationconfig.h
gains the file name, the three group names and the fixed grey of the CT_GRAY
type. It also gains the shadow defaults, the frame defaults, the two pad limits
and readShade(). The decoration and the module read the same constants and the
same shade reader. The change moves code out of qtcurvedecorationconfig.cpp and
out of the anonymous namespace. It changes no value and no behaviour.

A save reaches a running compositor. save() sends the reloadConfig signal of
org.kde.KWin on the session bus, as kcm_breezedecoration does. KWin reloads its
configuration and emits the reconfigured signal of KDecoration3.
Decoration::reconfigure() then reads the two files again, drops the two shared
drop shadows and repaints. DecorationConfig::instance() gives a non const
reference for that call. The page therefore needs no restart of the compositor,
and the note of the page asks for none.

Every open window has a decoration, and each one gets that signal. The
configuration and the two shadows belong to the process, so the read and the two
resets must run one time for each signal. DecorationConfig gains a generation
counter, and reload() raises it. A decoration holds a copy of the counter.

The first decoration of a signal finds the same number, and it does the read and
the two resets. Each later decoration finds a larger number and reads nothing.
Every decoration then takes the shadow of its window state and repaints. Every
window therefore keeps the one shared shadow object.

The page is a port of qt6/kwinconfig/qtcurvekwinconfigwidget.ui and of
qt6/kwinconfig/qtcurvekwinconfig.cpp. qt6/kwinconfig/qtcurvekwinconfig.cpp
carries the LGPL notice of QtCurve. The user interface file carries no notice,
and it takes the licence of the tree. The port drops the widgets of the keys
that the KDecoration3 decoration never reads:

* Border size. The Window Decorations page of KWin holds the border size now,
  and KDecoration3 gives the value to the decoration.
* Remove from maximised windows, and window grouping. KWin holds the first one,
  and the second one left KWin with the KDE4 tab group.
* Active opacity, inactive opacity and the opaque border. The compositor draws
  the frame of a KDecoration3 plugin, so the plugin paints no opacity of its
  own.

The port replaces one rule of the KDE4 module and drops another. The KDE4
module forced the custom shadows box on for a border of the shadow shade.
Decoration::borderColor() reads the shadow colour for that shade with the box
off as well, so the box now stays as the user left it. The rule that replaces
it keeps the two colour rows live while the box is on, or while a border holds
the shadow shade. The box drives the size row and the two offset rows alone. The
user interface file therefore holds no enabled property and no signal connection
for the two group boxes, and setWidgetStates() holds every rule. The KDE4 module
also registered a D-Bus service to stop a second copy of the page. That page
lived inside the settings dialogue of the style as well. The new module is a
separate plugin, so it drops the service.

The port also drops the gradient box of the inactive shadow.
QtCurve::ShadowConfig reads the ShadowType key that the box wrote. The painter
of the KDecoration3 decoration draws every shadow in one colour, so the value
changed nothing for that decoration. The KWin page of kstyle_qtcurve6_config
still writes the key and reads it back into its own box. The module writes no
ShadowType key, and a save leaves the key of the KDE4 settings module as it is.

The patch adds no ctest entry for the module. kcmshell6 --smoke-test needs a
graphical platform. The constructor of KColorButton of KF6 WidgetsAddons 6.29
reads the clipboard, and the offscreen platform gives no mime data.
kcm_breezedecoration of Breeze 6.7.4 stops with the same fault on the offscreen
platform.

What the tests show:

* The metadata of the plugin is valid. The identifier is kcm_qtcurvedecoration,
  and KPluginFactory builds QtCurve::DecorationKCM from it.
* load() of an empty file gives the defaults of the decoration. The two borders
  are none, the round corners are on, the custom shadows are off and both pads
  are 0. The active shadow is 35 pixels in the focus colour. The inactive shadow
  is 30 pixels in the fixed grey.
* A file with OuterBorder=2, InnerBorder=1, RoundBottom=false, TitleBarPad=3,
  EdgePad=2, CustomShadows=true, ActiveShadows Size=45, HOffset=4, VOffset=8,
  ColorType=5 and Color=255,0,0 reaches every widget of the page. A second
  load() of the open module reads the same file again, so a save from the other
  page reaches the widgets.
* A page with a light outer border, a dark inner border, TitleBarPad=4 and
  EdgePad=3 writes those keys alone. The same page gives the active shadow 40
  pixels in red, a horizontal offset of 2 pixels and a vertical offset of 7
  pixels. It gives the inactive shadow 20 pixels. The file keeps its BorderSize
  key, its Grouping key and the two ShadowType keys of the KDE4 settings module.
  A file with no ShadowType key gets none.
* defaults() gives every widget the default of the decoration, and it writes
  nothing.
* An outer border of the shadow shade keeps the two colour rows live while the
  custom shadows box is off. The size row and the offset rows stay grey.
* The decoration of the saved file draws a title bar of 31 pixels and borders
  of 9 pixels. The active shadow image is 89 by 89 pixels. Its padding is 37,
  32, 41 and 46 pixels, and its strongest pixel is 255, 0, 0.
* A decoration that starts on an empty file draws borders of 4, 23, 4 and 4
  pixels. Its shadow is grey and 69 by 69 pixels. The reconfigured signal after
  a swap of the file gives the numbers of the saved file, so the reload works.
* Two decorations that share one settings object hold one shadow object. The
  reconfigured signal after a swap of the file gives both the numbers of the
  saved file, and both still hold one shadow object.
* save() puts the reloadConfig signal of org.kde.KWin on the session bus.
* kcmshell6 --smoke-test org.kde.kdecoration3.kcm/kcm_qtcurvedecoration returns
  0 on the xcb platform.

Drop this patch when QtCurve ships a KDecoration3 decoration upstream.

